 ### [Voxel Feature Composition](7021-voxel-feature-composition.md)
-Generates per-voxel feature vectors by sampling points within each Gaussian's volume and weighting them by Gaussian attenuation. It aggregates voxel-level geometric and appearance data into a single feature vector per voxel for storage or further processing. Unlike its sibling [Mean Feature Aggregation by Voxel](11242-mean-feature-aggregation-by-voxel.md), which relies on direct spatial averaging of raw points, this method computes features by sampling Gaussian volumes and weighting by attenuation.
+Generates per-voxel feature vectors by sampling points within each Gaussian's volume and weighting them by Gaussian attenuation. It aggregates voxel-level geometric and appearance data into a single feature vector per voxel for storage or further processing. Unlike its sibling [Mean Feature Aggregation by Voxel](11242-mean-feature-aggregation-by-voxel.md), which relies on direct spatial averaging of raw points, this method computes features by sampling Gaussian volumes and weighting by attenuation. Choose it when Gaussian-based attenuation weighting is required over simple averaging.
 
 ### [Attention Aggregation for 3D Points](12093-attention-aggregation-for-3d-points.md)
-Aggregates unordered, variable-sized sets of retrieved pixel features from multiple input views into a single unique feature vector for a query 3D point. It solves the problem of identifying which retrieved features are true descriptions of the query point in the presence of visual occlusions and arbitrary input counts. It sets itself apart from simple statistical aggregation (like mean pooling) by learning to weight views based on visual consistency and relative distance, rather than treating all views equally.
+Aggregates unordered, variable-sized sets of retrieved pixel features from multiple input views into a single unique feature vector for a query 3D point. It solves the problem of identifying which retrieved features are true descriptions of the query point in the presence of visual occlusions and arbitrary input counts. It sets itself apart from simple statistical aggregation (like mean pooling) by learning to weight views based on visual consistency and relative distance, rather than treating all views equally. Choose it when handling multi-view occlusions and variable input counts.
 
